theory.intro:
A vector's **components** say how far it moves across and how far it moves up. The same vector can be written three ways: as an ordered pair $(4,3)$, in component form $4\underset{\sim}{i}+3\underset{\sim}{j}$, or as a column $\begin{pmatrix}4\\3\end{pmatrix}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.5,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.5)--(0,4.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[->,thick,blue] (0.7,0.7)--(4.2,3.2) node[midway,above left,font=\footnotesize] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[dashed,gray] (0.7,0.7)--(4.2,0.7);
\draw[dashed,gray] (4.2,0.7)--(4.2,3.2);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (2.45,0.6) {$x$ across};
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (4.3,1.95) {$y$ up};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
Write the vector $\underset{\sim}{v}$ shown below as a column vector.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-2,-1) grid (5,4);
\draw[<->] (-2.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-1.5)--(0,4.5) node[above]{$y$};
\foreach \i in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\i,0){$\i$};
\foreach \i in {-1,1,2,3,4} \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,\i){$\i$};
\draw[->,thick,blue] (-1,1)--(4,3);
\node[above left,font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,2){$\underset{\sim}{v}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Write the vector $\underset{\sim}{w}$ shown below in component form.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-2,-2) grid (4,4);
\draw[<->] (-2.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.5)--(0,4.5) node[above]{$y$};
\foreach \i in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\i,0){$\i$};
\foreach \i in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,\i){$\i$};
\draw[->,thick,blue] (2,3)--(-1,-1);
\node[above left,font=\scriptsize] at (0.5,1){$\underset{\sim}{w}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

